    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The codex is a Maya screenfold book from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, probably from the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>, and scholars place its making between the 11th and 14th centuries.</li><li>Its pages hold accurate astronomical tables, notably for Venus and the Moon, together with ritual schedules, religious references, and information on seasons, illness and medicine.</li><li>It survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in 1739 and was damaged in the bombing of Dresden in 1945; it later played a key role in deciphering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maya-script">Maya hieroglyphs</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Dresden Codex is one of four hieroglyphic Maya codices that survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> in the New World. After it spent February 1945 in a flooded basement during the bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, the sheets 6/40, 7/39 and 8/38 were put back in reverse order when they were returned to their glass cabinet.</p></aside>
    </section>

